WEBVTT

00:00:34.795 --> 00:00:50.706
Recording in progress. Perfect. And I want to welcome you to the C.G. Jung Helpdesk meetup group, and I want to welcome you to today's special topic, which is the personal unconscious. It's a guide to not getting lost.

00:00:52.843 --> 00:01:12.541
And this has been a topic I've been looking forward to a very, very long time because There were several ideas of Jung and like general ideas that I had that connect in this topic. And it has to do with the experience that I made throughout.

00:01:15.452 --> 00:01:37.985
So very often you experience that people don't really listen. And I don't mean that in a negative way, but that people can be kind of distracted. So I'm in my daily life, I'm a team leader. I have, I have several team members and a normal job for a team leader is, for example, to delegate tasks.

00:01:38.578 --> 00:01:58.399
That means to say to someone, please do this because it is important. And it's really interesting to see when you try to explain something to someone, the understanding that they will take with them. And Very often what happens there is that people will take the thing, but kind of get distracted.

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:22.524
Suddenly they have, they get their own ideas, which is not necessarily bad, but at the same time, they don't really remember what you're saying. And this is an experience that I've made many times, not necessarily with my employees, but you can feel it sometimes that people kind of are distracted by themselves, but they're also kind of lost by themselves.

00:02:23.264 --> 00:02:32.529
Meaning they have a strange relationship to the world and the things that they're experiencing and that are happening around them. It's a very personal relationship.

00:02:35.997 --> 00:02:52.314
It's a way to be kind of isolated in their behavior, in their thinking, in their thoughts, and Sometimes you can also feel it for yourself, especially when someone tells you that you are kind of lost in yourself and the, the assessments of situations is not correct.

00:02:52.859 --> 00:03:14.386
And they're coming up all these strange ideas and one has a very emotional connection to things that make other people wonder, but also that makes one wonder about other people. So this is why I have this picture here from Alice in Wonderland. Where Alice is talking to the Smiling Cat and the Smiling Cat is saying, we are all mad here. And Alice's reaction is, at me?

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:35.297
Which is like humorous, but it's also on point for many things. Helping people to see things clearly. This is when we want to help someone. This is when someone has a problem or we point something out to someone and they struggle with that.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:04:00.718
Experience to get to the point where they kind of are not distracted by things. I have a very good friend and this friend is coming out of a relationship and he loved his girlfriend very much, but also the relationship was kind of weird. And of course we talked to him and of course we're trying to help him, but at the same time he's there.

00:04:01.797 --> 00:04:23.663
Very much distracted and very much in the, in the old relationship where you have to then have a little bit patience and stay with him, keep with him, and support him there because people have to work through things. And the question is then really, okay, what, what people need to work through? What does distract people? What does lead people in a kind of wrong direction?

00:04:25.868 --> 00:04:43.578
How they can they be sensitive? To, to certain topics. What, how do certain topics trigger them, as it's said very often today? And I think the connection there to Jung is the personal unconscious.

00:04:44.836 --> 00:05:09.361
And to say it then in Jung's own words, he's saying, the personal unconscious is a layer of contents that could be conscious just as well. It is perfectly superfluous to have a personal unconscious, a sort of negligence. Nobody should imagine they are different from others, or that they are perfectly moral or aesthetic, or any other illusion.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:30.824
Such people are incapable of realizing the impersonal unconscious— impersonal meaning the collective— quite naturally, because they are always in the dark and therefore never aware of it, Until the personal unconscious disappears. That is, as long as they have wrong theories, expectations, illusions about themselves or about the world.

00:05:31.782 --> 00:05:55.447
So Jung was a therapist and he worked as a therapist his whole life, and he was there to help people. And his style of therapy was the talking cure. So psychoanalysis, or the brand that he had, analytical psychology. Mm-hmm. So you sit down with people who have a problem and you talk it through with them.

00:05:56.234 --> 00:06:16.288
And he would observe the patients and he would see how they, how they would react. And he would also see how issues would resolve and also how the focus can be weird. And I think this is something that everyone can connect to. Sometimes you have a person in front of you and that person is just mentally completely else.

00:06:16.806 --> 00:06:35.358
Completely unapproachable, and there seems to be no progress really to get them off the track, which cannot be a good track, and just be very, let's say, hurt or feeling like a victim.

00:06:36.451 --> 00:07:01.171
So what Jung would do with, uh, patients would talk through the problem, resolves them through speech. And this is back then was revolutionary because it did not have to do anything with biological reasons. This was a thought before, before the thought was the brain is damaged and it's a kind of mechanical problem. The car has a broken engine, so it cannot drive.

00:07:01.428 --> 00:07:17.691
So it does not make sense to do anything with a car because how to fix a brain mechanically? And Freud has proven together with His colleagues, Jung, Adler, and so on, that it's possible to talk with people through problems. And then the question is really, what changes?

00:07:17.803 --> 00:07:42.004
What can mentally change in a human being that it suddenly loses interest in the wrong things, suddenly sees things clearly, understands things clearly, can move ahead in their life, in their relationships, in the right direction? If it's the same person, it's the same life, but like the understanding is a different one. How's that possible and what did happen before?

00:07:43.594 --> 00:08:07.023
And the question of the personal unconscious is of course like, how is it personal? Jung says we have a consciousness. This is our center of attention. This is what we know, what we can do, what's familiar to us. And we have our, everything that's else in the world and exists is just unconscious. We are not aware of it. We don't have an understanding.

00:08:07.071 --> 00:08:29.904
It's not connected to us somehow. Like things that are happening on the other side of the world are unconscious. The thing, to me at least, the things that are happening in the person in front of me, unconscious. So a lot of things that's happening in the background of my mind, unconscious. So Jung says we have a personal layer there.

00:08:30.593 --> 00:08:41.422
A lot of people know about the collective unconscious. That means the places where the archetypes are, where symbols are, where human culture is. Where creative ideas are coming from, but we have this layer of the personal unconscious.

00:08:41.936 --> 00:09:05.622
And he says, the personal unconscious contains everything forgotten or repressed or otherwise subliminal that has been acquired by the individual consciously or unconsciously. This material has an unmistakably personal stamp. And also, a more or less superficial layer of the unconscious is undoubtedly personal. I call it the personal unconscious.

00:09:06.107 --> 00:09:29.109
The contents of the personal unconscious are chiefly the feeling-toned complexes, as they are called. They constitute the personal and private side of psychic life. This connects him already to Freud because this is actually a quite Freudian idea, especially when we look here at the words of repressed or forgotten.

00:09:30.748 --> 00:09:54.689
For Freud, the unconscious was a kind of byproduct. Freud would structure the psyche as the ego. So that's you, the superego, that would be kind of the rules of society brought to you by the upbringing through the parents, and then the id, that would be then the primal drives, sex, hunger, murderous lust, all these different things.

00:09:55.447 --> 00:10:17.715
And of course, they would not be possible to bring into communion or bring it together with the rules of society. So they get repressed and they get put in a place. They come up, they're being pushed down, and where are they being pushed to? They're being pushed in the unconscious. So for Freud, the unconscious is a little bit like an attic or cellar.

00:10:17.795 --> 00:11:00.706
This is where you store the stuff that you don't want to have around in your normal apartment and in your normal life. Jung looked at that and Jung said, oh, that can't be all there is to the unconscious. There's more happening there in the psyche. And he would have this idea of the collective unconscious. So like a kind of where the basements of all the people are connected.

00:11:01.886 --> 00:11:21.954
And the interesting thing here is that while Jung and Freud worked together for a long time and then had a breakup. Jung would still use many ideas from Freud because Freud was right in many, many, many ways. And it has to do with the personal unconscious.

00:11:22.147 --> 00:11:45.558
It's a thing that could be as well in being conscious, but they're forgotten or they're repressed. There can be traumatic events. That can be things we don't want to know about ourselves, things that are uncomfortable. And this is already a very strong indication, like what ends up in the personal unconscious. And it has to do with a feeling-toned complex. What is a complex?

00:11:45.654 --> 00:11:53.533
I will go into later, but the important part is here really feeling, emotion, a strong emotional reaction to things.

00:11:54.216 --> 00:12:10.910
That means anything that feels kind of strong in its experience that could be especially anxiety, that could be fear, that could be anger, that could be anything that kind of overwhelms us.

00:12:11.872 --> 00:12:31.554
So Jung says the psyche has a kind of protection mechanism that the consciousness knows what it can process. A little bit like your body knows what to process food-wise, meaning if you eat something, your body will tell you whether it likes it or not. If it It's like it tastes good, then everything was fine.

00:12:31.906 --> 00:12:52.335
But if it does not taste well, for example, when it's bitter, that's normally a sign in nature that something is poisonous and you should spit it out. And also at a certain point, your stomach will just reject it and you will puke it out. So you will feel it in your body if there's something you cannot really process. And he says the consciousness in a certain way is the same.

00:12:52.832 --> 00:13:13.055
It knows what kind of experiences, what kind of impressions, images. It can process, what it can handle, and everything that it cannot handle, it will put kind of in the personal unconscious. It will be kind of repressed. It will be kind of ignored because it's too hot. It's too frightening. It's really steering away the eyes to not deal with it.

00:13:13.761 --> 00:13:37.266
But Jung says this has a very negative effect because you're building something in your unconscious and it does not really go away. It's a cellar in the attic. It's getting Fuller and fuller, and it's starting to act up. It's starting to complain. So how does it form? How does the personal unconscious come into existence?

00:13:37.443 --> 00:14:00.513
Because Jung says, when we start out life, we don't really have that. He says, when we are born as children, we are conscious, very little, not so much. And there's a collective unconscious. Meaning there's an immediate experience of what is collective. What is collective meaning? What is part of human nature?

00:14:00.562 --> 00:14:19.709
What is a part of human experience that we all share? The part of human psychology that we all share. And Jung looked a lot into children's psychology and he noted like children have amazing archetypal dreams without any understanding of it. Because they don't have now the cultural background to understand it.

00:14:20.404 --> 00:14:35.696
But he says children very often have a clearer view on things because they don't have anything that gets filtered through their personal unconscious. They don't have anything that might skew the view or veil it in some way.

00:14:36.583 --> 00:15:00.612
So he says, we are going to life, we growing up, we make experience, having things that we cannot deal with or that are uncomfortable, that have a too strong emotional reaction for us. And this layer starts to form, and it is about the things that we do not attend to, that we kind of forgot, or that are repressed or traumatic.

00:15:03.352 --> 00:15:26.188
And this is what Jung talks about when he talks about shadow integration. Like the shadow forms there in the personal unconscious, and the fact can be That if you do not integrate the things, the meaning they're not part of your consciousness, you will carry them around as baggage and they will just accumulate and become bigger and bigger and bigger.

00:15:28.554 --> 00:15:48.908
So what is the effect of the personal unconscious on our daily life, on our consciousness? And he's saying, you see, the perception of the unconscious becomes uncertain the moment you begin to repress things. It is repression which produces the veil of the personal unconscious, that layer between the conscious and the collective unconscious.

00:15:49.742 --> 00:16:13.552
As soon as the personal unconscious comes in between with repressions, motives, personal preferences, moods, reservations, and so on, then you are quite uncertain of what you have seen. Perhaps you made it up. There's a tendency to mold things, to change the vision according to fears or expectations. According to ideas of how things ought to be.

00:16:16.304 --> 00:16:39.847
So it is not only that you have a kind of baggage that you're carrying around with you, but it starts clouding your vision. It starts influencing how you see things. There's many, many different ways how to experience the world and how to discuss about something. Like we do that a lot. We talk about movies, we talk about stories. Yeah. We talk about news from all over the world.

00:16:40.184 --> 00:16:54.847
We talk about things that happen. We talk about gossip. And it's interesting to see when you have different people, they have different opinions because they paid attention to different things. One thing can be, of course, their personality, but another thing can be a kind of personal baggage.

00:16:56.020 --> 00:17:13.265
And you can develop the skill of that to seeing that where people are, they make it about themselves, even when it is not about them. But it touches something inside of them that feels very close and it feels very personal. And for Jung, that is the personal unconscious.

00:17:14.099 --> 00:17:35.752
And it happens that you walking around with the wrong glasses, basically, or you drive in a car, but your windshield is not clean. So you kind of navigate the world, but suddenly you get in all these collisions. Suddenly you have all these negative experiences. Yeah. And at the same time you think, oh, that's kind of because people don't like me.

00:17:35.947 --> 00:17:58.885
It's like, oh, the other people are at fault. So I also know some people who are absolutely convinced that their whole life they always got the short end of the stick and they're always the victim and people were always out to get them. And the question is then, okay, this is entirely possible, but at the other side is like, okay, well, Are you sure that's only about you?

00:17:59.386 --> 00:18:21.928
So this is what the personal unconscious is about and why Jung talks about it and why I wanted to talk about it. It's this idea of, okay, if you, if you go somewhere and it always stinks like poop, it could be you. But this event is not only about you, but it's also about understanding other people and also then learning how to deal with that. Yeah.

00:18:23.027 --> 00:18:40.776
So what Jung sees there as a culprit in the collect— in the personal unconscious are complexes. And complexes are separate psychological entities that work autonomously. They have a kind of worldview. They have a kind of action.

00:18:41.435 --> 00:19:04.819
They're, as he calls it, an autonomous group of associations that have a tendency to move by themselves, to live their own life. Apart from our attention. This is one great insight of psychology and psychoanalysis. We are not only our will, we are not only our consciousness. We do things, but there's some sabotaging happening.

00:19:05.333 --> 00:19:25.063
There's some, we get distracted, we don't think about certain things, we get the wrong ideas, we are focused on the completely wrong things. So sabotaging, but it happens in the background. And he says, this is a complex. He says, every constellation of a complex postulates a disturbed state of consciousness.

00:19:26.219 --> 00:19:49.458
The unity of consciousness is disrupted and the intentions of the will are impeded or made impossible. An active complex puts us momentarily under a state of duress, of compulsive thinking and acting, and behaves like an animated foreign body in the sphere of consciousness. So what Jung says is people lose themselves.

00:19:50.035 --> 00:20:09.936
They, they are not themselves anymore. They're only an emotion. They're only a thought. This is why I have here this image of the fireworks. You set someone off and they're then just this whole display of emotions and are unavailable. And it happens to oneself too. And one can be completely blind about that.

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:29.970
Jung had One patient that would go off the rail and he would record her when she would go off the rail and he would give it to her afterwards and say like, here, I recorded you. And she said, oh, that's not me. I never said that. And Jung said, this is a kind of pervasive thing about complexes. People don't really remember what they said.

00:20:30.243 --> 00:20:54.313
People don't remember how they acted and how they think what they said and how they behaved. They think It was perfectly normal and measured, but the outside view, people are shocked and concerned. So Jung calls a complex a mousetrap. For instance, you can talk quite sensibly with a person. The moment you touch upon the complex, it's all over.

00:20:54.876 --> 00:21:16.776
He is a victim of these silly ideas and turns around in circles. The complexes inhibit and sterilize man and make him monomaniac. So people become very touchy, very sensitive. They're blundering, they make mistakes, they become defensive, they become tyrannizing. And what he noticed, people have completely delayed reactions.

00:21:17.549 --> 00:21:40.806
Meaning, you might say something, but the person just does not react, or just way later, because they're kind of on rails. They're living through a program. They're playing a record. They're just explosive that it's gone up and it's now doing its thing. There's a complete loss of reason. It's incredibly hard then to talk because you're not talking to the person.

00:21:41.353 --> 00:22:00.395
There's something between the person and you, and this is the complex that's living in the personal unconscious. So he's saying it's not only that these contents go into the personal unconscious because they're forgotten or they're repressed or so on, they start to accumulate. And they become a psychological force through that. It becomes more bigger and bigger.

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:21.842
Example, when somebody has a complex about his intellect, for example, because he might think he's dumb, people will start behaving in very peculiar ways. He said these people would start to use big words. They would try to make an impression. They would try to convince people that they are very smart.

00:22:22.373 --> 00:22:38.535
Because they really, really, really don't want people to think they're not smart because it's too hurtful to hurt that they're not smart would set them off and it becomes overwhelming for them. And it is a kind of defense mechanism in that way.

00:22:40.300 --> 00:23:02.467
So they're managing their emotions, but this is then what it all comes to when you have an overbearing personal unconscious of un— Processed unconscious contents, it becomes just managing emotions to kind of be in a completely defensive state your whole life. And the other person too. It's an incredibly stressful thing to be in.

00:23:03.898 --> 00:23:22.751
It is constant protection, protection, protection, and living your life around something you cannot control because complexes you cannot really directly control. You can resolve them. Yeah. But when they catch you and they come over you, this is when people are lost and they feel it.

00:23:25.150 --> 00:23:44.900
So what is the effect of the personal unconscious and what is the consequence of that when people are distracted by themselves and the things they have not attended to and the complexes that come over them? Jung says there's a stunted development. People get kind of frozen in time. They do not move forward because he says there's a kind of balance in the psyche. Mm-hmm.

00:23:45.230 --> 00:24:03.373
He says our personality and how we are is already in the unconscious. We do not get born as fully formed personalities. All the stuff that we are and could be, it's still in the unconscious, but it needs to kind of reach us. But it, it can't because it has to go through this layer of the personal unconscious.

00:24:04.127 --> 00:24:22.857
And everything that comes up there goes then through this minefield of things. And this is when people then start to To freak out. So, and this is where they do not develop. Like the future you is not materializing because they're distracted by these problems of the past that have not been resolved.

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:48.831
So it's what we talked about before, that's the wrong assessment of situations, that it very much becomes about feelings and emotions and how they're being managed, especially negative ones. Mm-hmm. The inability to sit with discomfort or like criticism, even things that are just perceived as criticism, because this is the kind of pervasive thing. Everything becomes personal.

00:24:49.556 --> 00:25:13.304
And in that sense, everything feels like an attack, but it's in the psyche of the person. So even things that are completely unrelated to that. So the consequence of that is that it's then very, very hard to form relationships. So it's isolating. It isolates people. And he says, it lies in the nature of complexes that they separate individuals from one another.

00:25:14.155 --> 00:25:37.281
Complexes are utterly inhuman things, and it is quite impossible to reach any understanding of others with their help. Just as a complex is an independent quantity per se, the person affected is thus separated from others. Complexes always make us peculiar. And then unless we pull ourselves together, that's all we will be. So we become strange.

00:25:37.745 --> 00:25:51.943
We pick up strange ideas, strange habits, and we become less approachable for people. We become less understandable because in a sense we are less collective. For Jung, collective, that is not a negative thing. It means you're understandable.

00:25:52.504 --> 00:26:03.574
You are, you're connected to other people, but if you're isolated, As I've shown in the picture here, from the collective unconscious and the collective itself, they're disconnected from other people.

00:26:06.177 --> 00:26:28.076
So people become an incredibly hard puzzle to solve, and that makes it then hard to create a relationship with people because people want to have relationships with people that are fun to be around, that are helpful, that are nice, that are relaxing. But If an interaction becomes only about managing the emotions of the other person, it's incredibly strenuous.

00:26:28.446 --> 00:26:51.584
It's incredibly complex, but also for the other side, it's then very hard to form relationships and they wonder why and they think, oh, it has to do with me. Possibly. Possibly. So what is the relationship to the collective unconscious or the psyche and the unconscious in general? He says there's one very pervasive trap in there also.

00:26:52.146 --> 00:27:11.356
As I said, things go through the personal unconscious from the collective unconscious, and he says there are certain things that are collective, that are normal human experiences, normal human instincts that come up, but they're being perceived through a personal lens. This is what I mean with the perception is completely changed. It's veiled, it's distorted.

00:27:12.625 --> 00:27:25.228
He says a collective problem is If not recognized as such, always appears as a personal problem, and in individual cases may give the impression that something is out of order in the realm of the personal psyche.

00:27:26.103 --> 00:27:50.291
Meaning archetypes are about universal human experiences, and that can be loss of loved ones, that can be falling in love, that's falling out of love, that is being Successful, not being successful, forming a family, becoming part of a group, starting your own group. All these different things are universal experience.

00:27:50.339 --> 00:28:09.158
We have stories that are thousands and thousands of years old, and we're all confronted with them in a way of suffering, but also of redemption. And the problem is when it's being brought down to the personal level. That's the completely wrong approach.

00:28:10.200 --> 00:28:26.680
Meaning people think that universally human experiences are something that is wrong with them. And this is what Jung would do a lot with his patients. He would sit down with the patients and trying to reconnect them to humanity, to explain to them that the problems that they're having, they're not the only ones that having them.

00:28:27.818 --> 00:28:46.031
Meaning he would experience that, that people, would get isolated from their friends, from their family, because they become peculiar. They had experience that they cannot really explain. They think there's something wrong with them. The whole world feels like they're, it is against them.

00:28:46.949 --> 00:29:09.007
And he would try to reconceptualize the experiences of them to say, it's not only you. The psyche is not only you. There's another part. There's a whole of human history inside of you. And some things, this is just life. This is just how you should, what you should expect to happen. And you're not alone.

00:29:09.056 --> 00:29:32.779
There's a lot of people that have the same and the similar experiences. And even that, telling that to people and letting them know that they're not alone and their problems are not only theirs is a huge relief. It's a huge relief. It's reconnecting to the, to the collective. And the kind of perverse thing is the psyche always tries to rebalance itself.

00:29:32.972 --> 00:29:50.698
So when it notices that consciousness is completely one-sided, it's only one thing and too much stuff is in the personal unconscious, it will try to counteract. It tries to then give the experiences and the images and the symbols and the dreams and the fantasies and the hallucinations and all these other things.

00:29:52.063 --> 00:30:12.527
To bring the person back to the middle, to bring it back where it should be. But that at the same time looks very, very frightening. This is when in dreams animals try to catch you. It's a misunderstanding. It's a wrong perception. The animal tries to catch you to reintegrate with you because there's a part that wants your attention.

00:30:13.058 --> 00:30:23.915
But of course, people run away because they say it's, oh, it's very frightening. And it looks frightening because it's coming through the personal unconscious and it's not being recognized as something that is a normal part of the experience.

00:30:26.440 --> 00:30:47.894
So he's saying, the more there is of the personal unconscious, the more the collective unconscious forces itself upon you in a helpful attempt, but at the same time misunderstood event creating their problems. If the personal unconscious is cleared up, there's no particular pressure. And you will not be terrorized. You stay alone, read, walk, smoke, nothing happens.

00:30:49.035 --> 00:31:10.926
All is just so. You are right with the world. Jung sees the psyche as a, as a network, as a system that tries to be in harmony with everything, meaning harmony with consciousness, with the unconscious, with the surrounding world, with instincts, with archetypes, and so on. It's a balancing act. Everything is working together and needs to work together.

00:31:11.712 --> 00:31:30.451
The problem is when one thing starts shutting down, and that's then very often a part of the consciousness, meaning there's a way blocked. There's something not confronted. There's an experience that was not processed. There's an emotion that was too strong and is being repressed. There's this thought that feels too dangerous to entertain.

00:31:31.846 --> 00:31:54.395
And then this whole network stops, the whole flow of things stops, and then it starts to accumulate, and then it pressures, and it pressures down on the person. And it's a kind of attempt of the psyche to get the person to pay attention, but at the same time, it feels even more pressure. So that makes it then very, very hard.

00:31:54.877 --> 00:32:17.877
He would sit his patients down and explain the psyche and how it works, how things are flowing, how things are connected, and how, where things are blocking. And it's especially these personal things that feel incredibly blocking. And that are attached with incredibly strong emotions. So how to solve it? How to solve a complex?

00:32:18.136 --> 00:32:37.493
How to go through a complex and go to the other side? Because a complex itself, it's natural, but it feels like the dragon you have to confront. And he says, what analysis does then is first a reduction. It is analyzing your attitude.

00:32:38.120 --> 00:33:00.549
You must become conscious of many resistances and personal things which suppress your genuine mental activity or your psychological processes. All these inhibitions are so many impurities, and your mind must be purified before the psychological process of transformation can begin. So there's no other way around it.

00:33:01.046 --> 00:33:24.498
You have to turn around, confront the dog that is chasing you, and deal with it. Sit with the emotion, experiencing it fully. He says a complex can only be resolved by living it out completely. So to have the thoughts that are coming up, to have the emotions that are coming up, but at the same time to be careful that they not take you over.

00:33:25.844 --> 00:33:44.580
There is Something psychologically happening when talking about that and talking about them in a safe environment. This is why personal relationships are so important because they allow you to experience something that is very, very frightening that's happening inside of you.

00:33:45.615 --> 00:34:09.014
But if you have someone, and everybody can be very, very happy and lucky when they have someone like that. That they can talk with through tough situations, tough emotions, and that tells them the truth, but still sticks with them even when they see how difficult and peculiar that person can be.

00:34:10.970 --> 00:34:28.966
So it is an acknowledgement. It's the acknowledgement that the problems are from the personal unconscious. So there's just things that Maybe a person has not done, but at the same time, not having too much pressure and giving too much guilt about it because it is a natural process.

00:34:31.242 --> 00:34:47.655
And what, like, the amazing idea of psychoanalysis is, and analytical psychology, to say there are things in your psyche that are not you. Because Jung says this is a fallacy that a lot of people do. They think everything in our psyche is me. And he's saying, no, no, no, there are things that have nothing to do with you. Yeah.

00:34:48.108 --> 00:34:58.642
Their archetypes, their instincts, their complexes, and you have to distance yourself from them. You should not identify with them. You should say those are there, but it does not mean that I am them.

00:34:59.174 --> 00:35:21.364
People, of course, are personally involved with the things that are happening in their psyche, but they need to kind of create a metacognition about that, observe themselves and separate themselves out and create an emotional distance to things that they're experiencing, which is incredibly hard, incredibly, incredibly hard, and takes incredibly long amount of time.

00:35:21.460 --> 00:35:42.688
But it's, it's the only way how to do it. Distance yourself, but then allow these things to emerge. He says people have defensive strategies, and we talked about recently a book discussion about on psychic energy. And he says people sometimes have really weird ideas. And they're very, very much focused about certain things.

00:35:43.121 --> 00:36:03.480
You can ask a person a question, but the person will not answer the question. They will immediately start talking about something else. And the thing is, this thing that they pay attention to is kind of their protective barrier. They are, by investing all the time and energy into there, they're keeping away the energy from things that are too hurtful.

00:36:04.475 --> 00:36:23.328
So they kind of They don't even try to evade, but they also try to distract for themselves. So he's saying complexes can disappear through atonement or confession, either by the patient resuming a reasonable lifestyle or through reintegration into the community.

00:36:24.861 --> 00:36:41.307
He says human beings are smart and human beings been around for a very long time and through the ages and through the cultures. There have been different instruments how to help people with these psychological pressures and the problems that they're having.

00:36:41.709 --> 00:37:03.692
And he said, the, the Catholic faith, the idea of confession to talk about these things and to atone for them has a purifying mental effect. At the same time, we are not as religious as we were in the past. We now have the same thing. We kind of rediscovered the same thing.

00:37:04.547 --> 00:37:26.711
We have analysis and therapy and coaching and all these different things that allows people to talk again through problems, resolves them, but also to bring these topics to the table on a safe environment, in a nonjudgmental environment, in a forgiving environment. So it does not mean the only way to resolve complexes Confessions in a church.

00:37:27.631 --> 00:37:49.772
But the environment for that is that to take responsibility, to acknowledge, but also to distance and to rectify. And do that, do that for the things that are emotionally pressing on you and the thoughts that are overbearing. And this is then a journey.

00:37:49.836 --> 00:38:09.887
And Jung says this is This is part of the journey. This is a part of personal development. This is life. So this is an important thing to understand that nothing about this is unusual and nobody is free from that. Nobody is alone in having a personal unconscious, and that's where it's collective also. Again, everybody has complexes. It's normal.

00:38:09.936 --> 00:38:33.451
It's normal to have a personal unconscious. The problem is when it becomes too strong and it becomes pathological and becomes part Of the daily struggles that one has. So he says, our attempts to advance from our known consciousness behind the I and into the unconscious, whereby one first reaches the layer of the personal unconscious.

00:38:34.294 --> 00:38:56.283
There one spends a long time. One usually turns around on the surface and approaches the deeper layers in a spiral fashion. This involves the danger of losing oneself in the maze. Walking around in circles and discovering nothing but the personal. And this is the problem that we have. You live in a world and this world is incredibly complex and incredibly rich.

00:38:56.605 --> 00:39:17.200
There's amazing things to experience. But if you're trapped in your own personal unconscious, you don't see any of that. You're blind. You're blind to the world. You're blind to the beauty of the world. You're blind to anything that's happening because you think Everything is kind of personal and everything is a defensive posture that you have to the world.

00:39:17.539 --> 00:39:36.719
So it's not possible to be enriched by the world because you're not really seeing it. The wonders of the world are kind of hidden behind a veil. And this is very often what happens in the first part of life. Jung sees the life in different stages, similar as we are born, we are a certain way.

00:39:37.302 --> 00:39:56.851
We are a certain way till the midpoint of our life, but he says it's around middle of 30 or end of 30 where we're very concerned with these things, with the personal unconscious, but also with the world at large to establish ourselves, to have a career, to have a family, to have status, to learn the rules of society, to participate in society, all these different things.

00:39:57.365 --> 00:40:20.609
And it's not yet so psychological. It's kind of you're traveling and you're accumulating. He's saying the Biggest part then happens in the second part of life because this is then when people realize what worked in their life and what did not work. And psychologically, they shift and turn more inward and trying there to resolve things. Again, this is a normal part of life.

00:40:21.186 --> 00:40:44.616
Suffering is always with us, but we can kind of choose our suffering. And the heaviest burden we have is ourselves. But again, this is just part of normal life. So what does it mean? What does it mean to come out at the other side of such a development? He says, when you are finished with the personal unconscious, you are finished with the causal influence of the past.

00:40:45.017 --> 00:40:58.715
So the things in the past you have not taken care of and that were repressed or were forgotten. Then you must come to the reconstructive side, and the collective unconscious will speak in images, and the consciousness of unconscious objects will begin.

00:40:59.935 --> 00:41:15.715
If you can succeed in breaking down that dividing wall made by the personal unconscious, the shadow can be united with the ego, and the individual becomes a mediator between two worlds. The animus or the anima begins to be active now, and the anima will bring in the figure of the old man.

00:41:16.614 --> 00:41:32.798
All these figures will be projected into the conscious external world, and the objects of the unconscious begins to correspond to objects in the external world. So that the latter, the real objects, take a mythological character. This means an enormous enrichment in life.

00:41:33.970 --> 00:41:55.022
The collective unconscious, as I said, this is where art is, where culture is, mythology, religion, spirituality. And people are blocked in their experience of that if they have not cleaned house, when the cellar is too full, when the attic is too full, and these things are not resolved. So they're kind of missing a whole part in life. That's what I meant with stuck.

00:41:55.792 --> 00:42:20.268
They're kind of in an immature state as children caught in the middle. And this sounds now pretty harsh, but this is kind of what psychologically is happening there. So the world has a lot to offer, but it's not available. It's not being seen, and it does not reach the person because it cannot get through that wall. But this is where Jung says, this is psychological life.

00:42:20.348 --> 00:42:41.735
This is the symbolic life. This is a meaningful life. This is where this is happening. And to conclude everything, I have a last quote from Jung, which is, the more we become conscious of ourselves through self-knowledge and act accordingly, the more the layer of the personal unconscious that is superimposed on the collective unconscious will be diminished.

00:42:42.313 --> 00:42:55.217
In this way, there arises a consciousness which no longer imprisoned in the petty, oversensitive, personal world of the ego, but participates freely in the wider world of objective interests.

00:42:56.435 --> 00:43:17.683
This widened consciousness is no longer the touchy egoistical bundle of personal wishes, fears, hopes, and ambitions, which always has to be compensated or corrected by unconscious counter-tendencies. Instead, it is a function of a relationship to the world of objects, turning the individual into an absolute binding, and indissoluble communion with the world at large.

00:43:18.792 --> 00:43:41.766
The complications arising at this stage are no longer egoistic wish fulfillments, but difficulties that concern others as much as oneself. Because this is a lot where the meaning of life happens. It's with other people. It's in the relationships with other people. And it's very, very hard to be in a relationship with other people and to experience other people as who they are.

00:43:41.847 --> 00:44:00.328
Yeah. When one is blinded by oneself. So this was connected to a lot of other Jungian ideas. As I said, it's really a network of ideas. So I talked about shadow. I talked about basically associations, what's triggering complexes.

00:44:00.940 --> 00:44:22.033
When one has complexes, one has a neurosis, and then one can go to therapy to deal with that. But essentially it's a part of life and it comes down really to attitude, what one has as a stance to the world and to oneself. What does one want and where is someone striving towards?

00:44:23.437 --> 00:44:39.546
And I can recommend some other events that deal with these topics, which is episode 12, Perception and Experience, episode 34, where I delve deeper into complexes. And episode 17, Shadow and Projection.

00:44:40.871 --> 00:44:54.325
And I would like to thank you a lot for your time and attention, and I hope you learned something, and I hope you had as much fun as I had.

